From 746b5e646ed6ba91a6a15798b672cb882e37c809 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Homies Dev <dev@homies.local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 06:29:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] fix: correct the dealbreaker vocabulary and extract the
 matching engine
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Discover feed was empty for most users, and had been since the
dealbreaker pre-filter was introduced.

The filter tested `roomieGender !== 'No preference'` to decide whether a
user had opted out of the roommate-gender constraint. The questionnaire
in EditProfile.js never produces that string for that field; its opt-out
is "Don't Care". So every student who answered the question with the most
natural answer had the constraint applied literally, matched nobody, and
saw "You've seen everyone" with zero cards. Students who skipped the
question saw a full feed. Reproduced against the real option strings:
"Don't Care" and "Same gender only" each filtered out 100% of candidates.

This was not a logic error. The filter was internally consistent. It was
a vocabulary error between two halves of the system that had no test
holding them together, which is exactly the seam §12 of the working
method says to cover.

Three changes:

1. The engine moves to lib/matching.js as pure functions with no
   dependency on mongoose or express, so it can be tested without a
   database. tests/matching.test.js covers it with 23 cases whose
   fixtures are copied verbatim from the questionnaire's option arrays.
   Testing it against invented fixtures would have passed while the live
   feed was empty, so the fixtures are the point.

2. Opt-out is recognised through a NO_CONSTRAINT list rather than one
   hardcoded string, and missing data on the candidate's side never
   excludes them. A brand new signup has an empty profile; if absent data
   could fail a stated preference, every new user would be invisible to
   every established user, which presents as "the app has no users".

3. Smoking is filtered on `roomieSmoke` (what you asked for) rather than
   `smoke` (your own habit). The old comparison meant a non-smoker who
   had explicitly answered "Smoker ok" still had every smoker hidden.

Rejected: folding the other eight roomie* preference fields into the
filter. They are collected and currently unused, but widening a hard
filter is what caused this bug, and an over-eager filter fails silently
and looks like a data problem. If they are to be used they belong in
scoring, which changes the documented weights and is a decision for the
author, not a fix.

The five categories and their weights are unchanged, so the README's
algorithm section remains accurate.

The three inherited test files are deleted. They required mocha, chai and
chai-http, none of which were in package.json or the lockfile, so
`npm test` exited with "mocha: not found" and the project had no working
gate at all. They also wrapped every assertion in
`.catch(err => console.log(err.message))`, so even once installed they
would have passed unconditionally, and they asserted a response shape
(res.body.user._id) this project's User model has not had for some time.
mocha is now a real devDependency and `npm test` runs.

Does not fix: nothing is tested against a live database. The route
handlers, auth middleware and booking flow have no coverage yet.
